er as opposed to determining  whether an injection is a cheat or not  end quote gamerdoc says that valve is  essentially saying that because  hackers are hiding cheating software  within normal  and usually safe third-party software  valve's just gonna ban  everything from trying to connect to cs  go it's not unusual because the reason  why  it's blocked it's because uh cheaters  are using really using these uh  vulnerable  softwares like they have some they have  vulnerable  locations in the software so obs for  instance you're able to hook onto obs  with a driver or whatever and be able to  cheat  through obs or you could cheat through  nvidia stuff  or you could cheat through uh discord's  overlay  that's the reason why they're blocking  it it's because it's not  the fault of the anti-sheet team it's  the fault of the company  not removing these vulnerable things now  trusted mode doesn't specifically tell  you  what third-party software is potentially  raising red flags  so unless you're literally only running  cs go  have fun trying to figure out what  applications you need to close  gamerduck described trusted mode as a  glorified pr  move in response to vanguard which is  riot games's  intrusive anti-cheat system that they  rolled out with the release of valerian  he said that vanguard operates at the  kernel level of your computer's  architecture  which basically means it's a level of  your computer software that the general  gamer  rarely if ever has to deal with and  according to gamerdoc it's here at the  kernel level where you'll find the most  sophisticated and hardest to spot  cheating software  and again according to him cs go's  trusted mode doesn't work on this level  at all  instead operating on a more surface  level basis  which he's told us in the past does  almost nothing to stop  hacking or cheating software from  getting into your computer and listen  these days  intrusive anti-cheat systems are out  there faceit has one  vanguard is one it's 